ediately expanding.\n\nUltimately, I want to choose between H9 and I9. Evaluating H9, I have directly blocked Black\'s vertical line. Evaluating I9, it gives a standard diagonal indirect opening. H9 seems robust enough for this initial situation.\n\nLet\'s double-check: H is column 8. Row 9 is right below Row 8.\n\nEssentially, I can pick a direct opening or indirect, but I select H9 as a robust standard response. I select I9. No, I choose H9.\n\nThe task is to choose a move. I will give the output as H9.\n\nBlack opens at H8.\nI need to defend, by placing adjacent.\nCandidate 1: H9 (Direct block).\nCandidate 2: I9 (Indirect block).\nCandidate 3: G8 (Direct block).\nI will choose H9.\n\nReasoning: Black has just played the first move in the center. White\'s best strategy is to play adjacent to this stone to contest the center control immediately. H9 is a solid, standard response that blocks Black\'s vertical development.\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 9}\n\nDouble-checking: H9 is empty. H8 is occupied. Valid!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The game has just begun with Black playing the first move at the center of the board (H8). White has no immediate threats to address. The standard strategy for the second move is to play close to the center stone to contest control and limit Black\'s expansion. Playing directly adjacent (orthogonally or diagonally) is the most effective approach.\n\nReason: Placing a stone at H9 is a classic and solid response to a center opening. It directly blocks Black\'s potential vertical line and keeps White\'s stone close to the center of action, allowing for both defensive blocking and future offensive setups.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 9}'}
