es not have an Open 3.\n\nA few other ideas come to mind. I could attempt an offensive play, but I don\'t see any immediate chances to create an unstoppable series. I need to avoid falling into a passive game. Playing J10 might look interesting.\n\nOkay, so I have identified the primary threats, and it\'s clear I must block one of these lines. I believe the better move is I7.  It directly blocks the diagonal line, and it prevents Black\'s horizontal threat from building into a lethal sequence. Playing this move does not create any immediately winning scenarios for me, but it prevents an immediate loss. I7 seems to be the play here because it is strong defensively, and makes a connection to my existing stones.\n\nSo, I7 is the move.\n\n**Final Answer:** {"col": "I", "row": 7}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The board situation shows Black with two threatening "Open 2" formations: a horizontal line at F7-G7 and a diagonal line at G9-H8. If Black is allowed to play at H7, they form a horizontal Open 3. If Black plays at I7, they form a diagonal Open 3. An Open 3 is a major threat as it forces the opponent to block, giving the attacker initiative. \n\nWhite\'s best move is to defend against these simultaneously. Playing at **I7** is the optimal choice. \n1.  **Blocks the Diagonal**: It directly occupies the key extension point for Black\'s diagonal G9-H8, preventing the formation of an Open 3.\n2.  **Mitigates the Horizontal**: While it doesn\'t occupy H7, it blocks the "far end" of the horizontal line. If Black later plays H7 to extend F7-G7, the resulting line F7-G7-H7 is blocked by White at I7. This restricts it to a "Sleeping 3" (blocked on one side), which is significantly less dangerous than an Open 3 (open on both ends).\n3.  **Shape**: I7 connects loosely with White\'s stone at I9 (vertical split) and creates knight\'s move connections with G8 and H9, strengthening White\'s presence in the center.\n\nReason: I7 blocks Black\'s diagonal threat directly and neutralizes the horizontal threat by capping its potential extension. This defensive move prevents Black from gaining a winning tempo and helps stabilize White\'s position.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 7}'}
