a "Live 3" (or Jump 3 depending on perspective, but effectively a line capable of becoming an Open 4).\n- If Black plays at **D7**, they will form a line of 4 stones: **D7-E7-F7-G7**.\n- The ends of this 4-stone line would be **C7** (empty) and **H7** (empty).\n- A 4-in-a-row with both ends open is called an **"Open 4"**. An Open 4 is a guaranteed win because even if the opponent blocks one side, the other side remains open to complete the 5.\n\nTherefore, Black has a lethal threat at D7. White **must** prevent this Open 4.\n- If White plays **H7**, Black can still play D7 to form a 4-in-a-row (D7-G7). Although this resulting 4 would be blocked on the right side by H7, White would be forced to block at C7 immediately. Allowing the opponent to form a 4 usually puts the defender at a severe disadvantage.\n- If White plays **D7**, it directly occupies the critical square that would make the line an Open 4. Furthermore, by playing D7, White sandwiches the Black stones between D7 and the existing White stone at I7 (with H7 empty), effectively neutralizing the threat of this row completely.\n\nWhite has no immediate winning threats of their own (no 3 or 4 in a row ready to be extended to a win). Thus, defense is the only priority.\n\nEvaluation: The move at D7 is mandatory to stop Black from creating an unstoppable Open 4. It neutralizes Black\'s primary threat on Row 7.\n\nReason: Black has stones at E7, F7, and G7. Playing at D7 creates an Open 4 (D7-G7 with C7 and H7 open), which ensures a win for Black. White must block at D7 to prevent this.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "D", "row": 7 }'}
