s my position, and contributes to a better stone layout.  D6 only blocks.\n\nNow, do Black have any other threats? Let me double-check. The row 7 stones E7, F7, G7: if Black plays H7, it is a dead 4. If Black plays H7, it will be bounded by D7 and I7, making it a dead 4 (not a threat). Similarly, row 8 has no threats, and col F or col G cannot connect to a long sequence.\nA double check on the "Live 2" at G7 and F8.\nIf Black plays E9, Black gets a Live 3. No.\nBut this E9-F8-G7 is not as big a threat as the E7-F8-G9.\n\nSo, the only major threat that *must* be blocked is the diagonal E7, F8, and G9, that will become a winning "Live 4".\n\nTherefore, I\'m choosing H10. I have to block, but I\'m blocking in a way that actively builds a strong structure for White. My reasoning is solid, and I\'m confident in this move.\n\nNow, final JSON structure and validation checks: {"col": "H", "row": 10} Let\'s do it!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The board shows a critical threat from Black. Black has formed a continuous diagonal line of three stones ("Live 3") at E7, F8, and G9. If left unchecked, Black will play at either D6 or H10 on the next turn to form a "Live 4" (four consecutive stones with open ends), which is an unstoppable winning state in Gomoku. Therefore, White\'s move is forced; White must block this diagonal immediately.\n\nThere are two options to block: D6 and H10.\n1.  **D6**: Blocks the threat and connects vertically with the White stone at D7. However, this location is on the periphery of the main stone cluster and offers limited offensive potential.\n2.  **H10**: Blocks the threat and places a stone adjacent to White\'s existing stones at H9 and I9. This move creates a vertical connection with H9 (though blocked below) and a diagonal connection with I9. By clustering stones in the H/I columns, White maintains better shape and potential for future attacks compared to the isolated blocking move at D6.\n\nReason: White must immediately block Black\'s "Live 3" diagonal threat (E7-F8-G9) to prevent an inevitable loss. H10 is the superior blocking choice over D6 because it not only neutralizes the threat but also integrates well with White\'s existing stones on the right side (H9, I9), improving White\'s local structure for future turns.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "H", "row": 10 }'}
